# HP Pro c640 Chromebook (Google Dratini / Hatch) Keyboard Top-Row Mapping
# Matches both standard Google Coreboot DMI strings and OEM HP DMI tables
#
# Scancodes measured on real Dratini hardware (2026-10-31):
#   EVIOCGKEYCODE /dev/input/event2 -> 0x3b..0x44 = keycode 59..68 (KEY_F1..F10)
#   EVIOCGKEY while pressing brightness/mute BEFORE the fix -> KEY_F6/F7/F8
#   (the firmware really sends F6-F8, i.e. scancodes 0x40-0x42)
# In other words this machine's top row emits the *standard AT* F1-F10
# scancodes, not the ChromeOS action scancodes (0xea/0xe9/0xe7/0x91/0x92/...)
# that 60-keyboard.hwdb-style snippets use for some other Chromebooks. Mapping
# 0x3b-0x44 is therefore what actually changes the behaviour here; the old
# 0xea-0xb0 block never matched a real key press and was dead code.
#
# NOTE: the pn*Hatch* line matches every Hatch-based Chromebook, not just this
# Dratini. Other Hatch boards may emit different (action) scancodes; capture
# yours with keyboard/tests/check-top-row-hwdb.py --listen before reusing this
# block, and add a model-specific stanza if they differ.

evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:svnGoogle*:pn*Dratini*:pvr*
evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:svnGoogle*:pn*dratini*:pvr*
evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:svnGoogle*:pn*Hatch*:pvr*
evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:svnHP*:pnHP*Pro*c640*Chromebook*:pvr*
evdev:atkbd:dmi:bvn*:bvr*:bd*:svnHP*:pn*c640*:pvr*
 KEYBOARD_KEY_3b=back
 KEYBOARD_KEY_3c=forward
 KEYBOARD_KEY_3d=refresh
 KEYBOARD_KEY_3e=f11
 KEYBOARD_KEY_3f=scale
 KEYBOARD_KEY_40=brightnessdown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_41=brightnessup
 KEYBOARD_KEY_42=mute
 KEYBOARD_KEY_43=volumedown
 KEYBOARD_KEY_44=volumeup
 KEYBOARD_KEY_db=leftmeta
